<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
        xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
        xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
        xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
        http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-3.5.xsd">

    <!--
        BE-POL (#368) — grant ROLE_PLATFORM_ADMIN to the MSSP operator identity.

        Background. PR #367 (BE-POL-GLOBAL-ADMIN) made ROLE_PLATFORM_ADMIN the ONLY role
        allowed to write GLOBAL, cross-tenant-readable agent-policy templates on an
        MSSP-scoped request, and changeset 20260924001_platform_admin_role.xml seeds the
        authority ROW in jhi_authority. But nothing GRANTS that authority to any account,
        so in MSSP mode GLOBAL template writes are fail-closed-impossible for everyone —
        over-restrictive (safe), but it blocks the intended "MSSP operator authors GLOBAL
        templates" workflow.

        What this does. For every user that already holds the MSSP operator authority
        MSSP_ADMIN, also grant ROLE_PLATFORM_ADMIN — via the established jhi_user_authority
        join path, the same mechanism SOC_MANAGER / ANALYST use. This maps the new platform
        authority onto the EXISTING MSSP operator identity and ONLY that identity:

          - A tenant ROLE_ADMIN never holds MSSP_ADMIN, so it never receives
            ROLE_PLATFORM_ADMIN here. The #367 escalation (a single tenant admin authoring
            policy every other tenant inherits) stays closed.
          - Single-tenant / on-prem ROLE_ADMIN is unaffected: it holds neither MSSP_ADMIN
            nor ROLE_PLATFORM_ADMIN, and its GLOBAL-write path stays governed by the
            config-flag equivalence (hivearmor.multitenancy.single-tenant-admin-global-write),
            which this changeset does not touch.

        The runtime complement. TokenProvider.getAuthentication derives ROLE_PLATFORM_ADMIN
        from MSSP_ADMIN at authority-reconstruction time, so an operator provisioned by an
        external IdP (whose authorities arrive in the JWT auth claim and never land in
        jhi_user_authority) is also covered. This changeset covers the DB-backed operator
        and persists the grant; the two together cover every provisioning route.

        Safety / idempotency.
          - MSSP_ADMIN is an externally-provisioned authority and is NOT seeded as a
            jhi_authority row on a fresh / single-tenant install. The preCondition requires
            the ROLE_PLATFORM_ADMIN authority row to exist AND at least one MSSP_ADMIN grant
            to exist; on a fresh install the second check is 0 and the changeset is marked
            ran without inserting anything (onFail=MARK_RAN) — no FK violation, no-op.
          - The INSERT ... SELECT is guarded by NOT EXISTS so re-running inserts nothing,
            and the jhi_user_authority primary key (user_id, authority_name) is a second
            backstop against duplicates.

        Mirrors AuthoritiesConstants.PLATFORM_ADMIN and the SOC-role / platform-admin-role
        seed convention. Guarded with preConditions so re-running is safe.
    -->
    <changeSet id="20260925001-platform-admin-operator-grant" author="hivearmor">
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <and>
                <sqlCheck expectedResult="1">
                    SELECT COUNT(*) FROM jhi_authority WHERE name = 'ROLE_PLATFORM_ADMIN'
                </sqlCheck>
                <not>
                    <sqlCheck expectedResult="0">
                        SELECT COUNT(*) FROM jhi_user_authority WHERE authority_name = 'MSSP_ADMIN'
                    </sqlCheck>
                </not>
            </and>
        </preConditions>

        <sql>
            INSERT INTO jhi_user_authority (user_id, authority_name)
            SELECT ua.user_id, 'ROLE_PLATFORM_ADMIN'
            FROM jhi_user_authority ua
            WHERE ua.authority_name = 'MSSP_ADMIN'
              AND NOT EXISTS (
                  SELECT 1 FROM jhi_user_authority existing
                  WHERE existing.user_id = ua.user_id
                    AND existing.authority_name = 'ROLE_PLATFORM_ADMIN'
              );
        </sql>

        <rollback>
            <!--
                Rollback removes ONLY the platform-admin grants this changeset could have
                added: those held by a user who also holds MSSP_ADMIN. A platform-admin
                grant on a user WITHOUT MSSP_ADMIN was not created here and is left intact.
            -->
            <sql>
                DELETE FROM jhi_user_authority pa
                WHERE pa.authority_name = 'ROLE_PLATFORM_ADMIN'
                  AND EXISTS (
                      SELECT 1 FROM jhi_user_authority ma
                      WHERE ma.user_id = pa.user_id
                        AND ma.authority_name = 'MSSP_ADMIN'
                  );
            </sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
